In this lesson, we'll look at volume and capacity of right prisms, starting with the big idea: volume equals the area of the cross-section multiplied by the length. A right prism is just a solid where you push the same flat shape along a straight line, and the shape never changes. So if you can find the area of that flat shape, the rest is easy. For common prisms, a rectangular prism uses width times height for the cross-section, so volume is width times height times length. A triangular prism uses half base times height for the cross-section, so volume is that times the length. A trapezoidal prism uses half the sum of the two parallel sides times height, all multiplied by the length. Now here's a quick distinction. Volume is how much three-dimensional space a solid takes up, measured in cubic units like cubic centimetres. Capacity is how much a container can hold, measured in litres or millilitres. The link is simple: one millilitre equals one cubic centimetre, and one litre equals a thousand cubic centimetres. So a five hundred cubic centimetre container holds five hundred millilitres, or half a litre. Now let's see it in action.